### Controls

**Negative control (seed against seed).** Two runs of the same pseudo-random generator on different seeds must not differ. Every seed pair of a comparison is tested on each hypothesis's own outcome with that hypothesis's test. The control passes for a hypothesis when every one of its pairs was computed and none has a p-value below alpha after Holm–Bonferroni correction over every seed-pair test of the study ({{ design.families.negative_control }} in all). A failed or missing control makes the hypothesis invalid (row 3). So does a control that is not accepted as one that could have failed. That is a seed pair whose paired test had fewer prompts than the floor stated in the Sample size section, or whose permutation test ran too few permutations to return a p-value below the corrected level. Such a control says nothing about the seeds. The decision matrix calls it a control that could not have failed, and the reason recorded is then negative\_control\_cannot\_fail.

**Positive control (biased sampler).** {% if levels.epsilon %}A seeded arm whose sampler is deliberately biased by ε = {{ levels.epsilon | md }} is compared with the seeded arms on each hypothesis's own outcome with that hypothesis's test. It is detected when its uncorrected p-value is below alpha. A hypothesis resolves to equivalent only where it was detected (row 6). The sample-size ratings do not cover this comparison. For each hypothesis that can resolve to equivalent, the list below states how far the biased arm must move the outcome to be detected with {{ levels.power | md }} power under the worst-case variance. How far a sampler bias of ε = {{ levels.epsilon | md }} moves an outcome is not known before the run, so whether the control will be detected is not rated. Where it is not detected, the hypothesis is inconclusive, not equivalent.{% else %}This study registers none, so no hypothesis can resolve to equivalent.{% endif %}
