## Sample size

The prompt is the unit of analysis of a paired test. Repetitions (k) sharpen the value of each prompt and add no units. Told cells do the same on the quantum side only: the told cells of a seeded run share one seed for each prompt and repetition, so the variance bound counts them as one value on the control side. With {{ design.families.difference }} difference {{ 'test' if design.families.difference == 1 else 'tests' }} at α = {{ levels.alpha | md }}, a paired test on fewer than {{ levels.n_floor }} prompts cannot reach significance at the first step of the correction, whatever the effect. Every number below was computed before any data existed, at the first and strictest step of the Holm–Bonferroni correction.
{% if control_floor is not none %}
The seed-against-seed control has a floor of its own. It is corrected over {{ design.families.negative_control }} {{ 'test' if design.families.negative_control == 1 else 'tests' }}, so a seed pair fails only at a p-value below α / {{ design.families.negative_control }}. A paired test on n prompts has no exact p-value below 2 / 2ⁿ. From {{ control_floor }} prompts on, that smallest p-value is at most half the level a seed pair must reach. With fewer prompts it is not certain that two seeds that differ as much as the outcome allows would fail the control, so the harness does not accept such a control: a seed pair of a paired test on fewer than {{ control_floor }} prompts counts as a control that could not have failed, and the hypothesis is invalid (row 3). {% if below_control_floor %}In this study the following plan fewer prompts than that and can only resolve to invalid: {{ below_control_floor | md }}.{% elif paired_count %}Every paired hypothesis of this study plans at least that many.{% else %}This study has no paired hypothesis.{% endif %} No number of prompts below this floor is rated above insufficient or recommended.
{% endif %}
| ID | Outcome | Model · sampling | Planned | Rating | Smallest difference detected with {{ levels.power | md }} power | Prompts recommended |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
{% for row in sample_size_rows -%}
| {{ row.plan.id | md }} | {{ row.plan.label | md }} | {{ row.cell | md }} | {{ row.planned | md }} | {{ row.rating | md }} | {% if row.detectable is none %}Unknown: no closed-form power for this test.{% else %}{{ row.detectable | md }}{% endif %} | {{ row.recommended | md }} |
{% endfor %}
Worst-case variance, no prompt-by-source heterogeneity; true power may be lower.

The variance bound takes every reply of the quantum arm as independent and counts the told cells of a seeded run as one value. Under those two facts it is the largest variance a bounded outcome can have.

The recommended number of prompts gives {{ levels.power | md }} power for a difference as large as the margin, or for a difference of {{ levels.reference_effect | md }} standard deviations where the outcome has no margin in its own units. The ratings mean:

- **sufficient**: at least {{ levels.power | md }} power for a difference as large as the registered margin, under the largest variance a bounded outcome can have.
- **marginal**: at least {{ levels.marginal_power | md }} power at the margin. For an outcome without a margin in its own units: a detectable difference of at most {{ levels.reference_effect | md }} standard deviations. Such an outcome is never rated sufficient.
- **insufficient**: less than that, or too few prompts to reach significance at all, or fewer prompts than the seed-against-seed control is asked for, as stated above.
- **unknown**: the test runs on pooled tokens and has no closed-form power.

A rating is not a verdict and changes no decision rule. With an insufficient or unknown rating, an inconclusive result is likely even if the sources differ.
{% if pooled %}
The following {{ 'is a test' if pooled_count == 1 else 'are tests' }} on pooled tokens: {{ pooled | md }}. Such a test has no margin and no effect-size threshold. Any corrected p-value below alpha resolves to effect, however small the difference, and the statistic is reported beside the verdict so that its size can be judged. A result that is not significant comes with no statement of the difference that could have been detected.
