## Arms and equivalence

The harness pins the same amplifier (`zscore_mean`), calibration (`0`), prefetch (`false`) and draw size (`10000` entropy bytes per token) across non-greedy qr-llm arms. Source, per-request seed and epsilon are the only intended differences in the sampling envelope. Prompts, per-card overrides and generations per prompt are matched. External provider pipelines and greedy controls cannot establish this equivalence. In this section "equivalence" means that the arms run the same sampling pipeline; it is not the verdict equivalent of the decision rules.{% if config.conditioning %}{{ "

" }}{% if config.conditioning.mode == "hash_truncate" %}Entropy conditioning: hashed. The quantum arm samples from hashed bytes: the server cuts its raw read into blocks of {{ config.conditioning.in_bytes }} bytes, hashes each block on its own with `{{ config.conditioning.hash }}` and serves the first {{ config.conditioning.keep_bytes }} bytes of each digest, so {{ config.conditioning.read_ratio() }} raw device bytes are read for every byte served. The served bytes are uniform whatever the device's own byte distribution is. Seeded and system pseudo-random arms are uniform already and are not hashed; the four `qr_conditioning_*` keys are therefore among the intended differences in the envelope. The server records the parameters and the sha256 of the raw block with every request.{% else %}Entropy conditioning: raw. The quantum arm samples from the bytes as the device measured them. Raw device bytes are not uniform, so the quantum arm differs from the pseudo-random arms by the device's byte statistics as well as by the source, and this study has no control matched for those statistics.{% endif %}{% endif %}

