## Stopping and exclusion rules

{{ plan.stopping_rule | md }} {{ plan.exclusion_rule | md }}

Missing paired data are not imputed; report denominators and exclusions per arm/card and restrict paired analyses to complete matching units. A hypothesis with fewer than {{ levels.min_completeness | md }} of its planned data analysed is invalid, not negative. No silent retries that replace observed outcomes. Scheduling timers are advisory; every transition into running requires explicit host start/resume. A stopped run is labelled incomplete, not a completed negative finding. Paid-for artifacts are retained or quarantined, never deleted.

The exclusion of error samples has one exception, stated under "Result checks" below: a sample whose attempt ended in an error, and which was generated again and completed after the host resumed the run, is analysed. A resume generates only samples that have no completed reply, so it replaces no observed outcome.

## Result checks

The harness checks every reply and its token records while the run stores them. On an eval that runs on the harness's own runner it also checks every score and every judgement. On an eval that runs through Inspect, Inspect scores and grades the samples itself, and the harness checks neither. This document describes version {{ result_checks.version }} of these checks. They are fixed by the harness and are the same for every arm and every study: no setting of this study changes them. No check asks a model whether a reply makes sense.

**A flag excludes nothing.** A flag marks a sample and changes nothing else. A flagged sample stays in every analysis exactly as an unflagged one, and no test or verdict reads a flag. The flags are counted for each arm and eval on the results page. The kinds of flag are:
{% for flag in result_checks.flags %}
- **{{ flag.label | md }}** ({{ flag.kind | md }}): {{ flag.sentence | md }}
{%- endfor %}

The checks do not detect the following: {{ result_checks.not_detected | md }}.

**A taint excludes.** Under the exclusion rule above, a tainted sample is left out of the clean analysis. A sample is tainted in these cases:
{% for taint in result_checks.taints %}
- {{ taint.kind | md }}: {{ taint.when | md }}.
{%- endfor %}

One taint is lifted by a completed reply. A {{ result_checks.lifted_taint | md }} taint does not exclude a sample that has a completed reply. That is the case when the run was resumed and the sample was generated again: the taint belongs to the attempt that failed, and the reply that completed is analysed. A sample that also carries another taint stays out, and a sample with no completed reply is not analysed.

**What stops a run.** A check stops the run when one of the following holds. Each rule is followed by what Resume, the host's action that continues the run, does after it.
{% for rule in result_checks.rules %}
- **{{ rule.label | md }}** ({{ rule.rule | md }}): {{ rule.sentence | md }} {{ rule.recovery | md }}
{%- endfor %}

A stop is a pause, not an abort. No new sample or judgement is started, those under way finish, and nothing that was stored is removed. The run does not continue by itself: the host resumes it or aborts it. No completed reply is generated again and no judge reply that was received is asked for again because of what it says, with one exception: where an eval reads the judge's reply with its own code and that code fails on a reply, the judgement counts as failed and is asked again on resume. Every stop is stored with the run, with its rule, its counts and the time at which the run was resumed, and the stops of a run are listed on the results page.

